WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.810
Film Courage: What are the biggest mistakes
you've made on the first day of shooting?

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:07.210
James Kicklighter, Filmmaker: I think some
of the biggest mistakes I've made on the first

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:12.550
day of shooting usually pertain to interpersonal
things.

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:16.960
At this point I kind of know what I need to
do to get something done technically.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:24.400
I know the story, I know all of the different
pieces that I need to deploy to hopefully

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:25.400
have a good film.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.689
The first day obviously sets the tone for
many of those things right?

00:00:28.689 --> 00:00:33.360
You have sometimes people you've worked with
hundreds of times and sometimes you've got

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:39.890
people that are brand new that you've never
seen before and so when you're doing that

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:48.219
I think you have to figure out how those relationships
that you have existing are going to mesh with

00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:50.210
those that are new.

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:55.400
And it's figuring out the interpersonal dynamics
of how these people tick, what makes them

00:00:55.400 --> 00:01:01.050
tick, deciding what they think is funny and
not funny, things like that.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:06.509
Because that tone you set on your set on the
first day, it does not matter what the film

00:01:06.509 --> 00:01:07.509
is.

00:01:07.509 --> 00:01:12.710
It can be anything you're shooting, an ad,
a documentary film, whatever.

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:18.399
That is going to set the tone with that person
that doesn't know you for the rest of the

00:01:18.399 --> 00:01:29.389
shoot and if you blow that by misjudging someone
by how they feel about things (whatever),

00:01:29.389 --> 00:01:35.390
then you've got to work on getting that back
(to get them back).

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:42.213
I think that may be for me personally the
most...that's something I messed up before.

00:01:42.213 --> 00:01:44.090
I try to do better about now.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:47.990
Film Courage: Well...that's big of you to
admit that you've misjudged.

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:54.359
But there are also people that look for a
challenge and they someone in charge or whatever

00:01:54.359 --> 00:01:59.450
and bring whatever they're dealing with and
they say "No...I'm going to be the thorn in

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:00.450
the side to this person."

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:01.450
James: Sure.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:03.659
And some people definitely have that.

00:02:03.659 --> 00:02:10.259
I try to meet as many people as possible across
as many departments as possible before shooting

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:13.860
something to kind of get a sense of people
and how they are.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:17.280
And if there's someone that I think is so
egregious, I'll talk to the department head

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:18.620
and say let's cut this person.

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:20.810
I've done it.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:29.200
And so that tone and climate in a set is very
important.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.849
That starts at the top, it starts with me.

00:02:31.849 --> 00:02:38.129
If you set a good tone, if you set a good
atmosphere for your cast, for your crew, typically

00:02:38.129 --> 00:02:42.850
even if you're working on a lower budget or
if something is not paid as well (whatever

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:51.030
it is), they will work better because they
like the space they're in.

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:54.579
And so it is on me as a director to set that
tone.

00:02:54.579 --> 00:03:00.939
If I'm heinous or if I come in with a bad
attitude that trickles down very fast.

00:03:00.939 --> 00:03:06.579
So it's really my job to figure out what makes
my crew and my cast tick.

00:03:06.579 --> 00:03:10.959
And once I understand that dynamics of that
then I have a lot more latitude to do the

00:03:10.959 --> 00:03:12.140
things as I want to.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:17.069
Film Courage: Yes, I don't know if I see you
being the one to start this...I'll just say

00:03:17.069 --> 00:03:18.069
this.

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:19.069
James: That's kind.

00:03:19.069 --> 00:03:21.579
I'm sure there are people who have worked
with me who don't like me.

00:03:21.579 --> 00:03:22.769
It happens to everyone else.

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:27.090
Film Courage: But sometimes people don't like
someone for something that is not the individual's

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:28.090
fault.

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:29.090
James: That's true.

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:30.090
Film Courage: And I'll be the one to say this.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:35.989
So if you can sense someone is trying to whatever...they
want to be the director and they are sort

00:03:35.989 --> 00:03:39.840
of like coming from the side and doing things
to usurp you, what's the best way to diffuse

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.359
some of this?

00:03:41.359 --> 00:03:43.709
Without having to call a department head and
send them home.

00:03:43.709 --> 00:03:47.580
Are there ways with humor, maybe no humor
to gain their respect?

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:52.099
James: Of course, well I try to figure out
what makes that person tick and sometimes

00:03:52.099 --> 00:03:57.580
it's just identifying little things like they
need this thing in craft services, right?

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:03.349
It's sometimes figuring out that they love
this type of coffee and having a PA go get

00:04:03.349 --> 00:04:05.319
it for them, whatever it may be.

00:04:05.319 --> 00:04:06.930
It's sometimes just little things.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:13.849
It's observations, so even a little observation
like that can go a long way to make someone

00:04:13.849 --> 00:04:17.160
feel wanted and needed on a set.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:22.300
Because I think that when people want to take
charge it's not usually because they are a

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:27.419
dominating bully on the set (it can be), usually
it's because they think you're not competent.

00:04:27.419 --> 00:04:32.500
I think that usually that's the reason that
people try to do that and so if you display

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:39.020
competency, if you're calm, if you know what
you want, this may be shocking to you but

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:46.810
I'm usually not hot-headed and so I keep a
very even tone even with myself.

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:49.550
Even if I'm not in a good mood or if I'm tired,
whatever it is.

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:55.210
I try to not project that onto the set because
I know that this will trickle down.

00:04:55.210 --> 00:05:01.750
So I try to find the things that that person
needs to feel comfortable on set even if it's

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:05.800
a creature comfort like a coffee or a crafty
thing.

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:10.669
But then it's just making sure that they understand
that I understand what I'm doing.

00:05:10.669 --> 00:05:14.819
I've been very lucky that I haven't had that
happen recently but earlier in my career,

00:05:14.819 --> 00:05:15.819
for sure.

00:05:15.819 --> 00:05:21.650
I definitely had to deal with that with actors
and with people working on the film, for sure.

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:27.460
And I've learned from those lessons and know
that I have to make sure that my stuff is

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:32.270
together before I can tell all of these people
what to do and how to do.

